<p>How do I test code in Bayan? Tests are part of the language: a <code>test</code> block, an <code>assert</code> statement, and <code>bayan test</code> to run them.</p>

<h2 id="unit-tests">Write and run unit tests</h2>
<p><strong>Runnable.</strong> A <code>test "name" { … }</code> block goes in the same file as the code, or in a sibling <code>x.test.byn</code>. <code>assert</code> is a statement, not a function, and a failed one prints the value of every sub-expression. There is no assertion library; plain expressions cover it. <code>assert f() fails E.case</code> checks that a call fails with a given error, so you do not write a <code>catch</code> and a <code>panic</code>. <code>@cases(…)</code> turns one test into a table: each row runs and is reported separately.</p>
<p>Functions that <code>fail</code> are errors as values, so a test calls them with <code>try</code> like any other code. <code>bayan run</code> ignores the tests and runs <code>main</code>; <code>bayan test</code> runs the tests.</p>
<example name="cookbook-testing-unit"/>
<p>Running <code>bayan test cookbook-testing-unit.byn</code> prints:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">running 5 tests in 1 module

◆ 5 passed · 0.42s</code></pre>
<p>Change <code>assert n == 50</code> to <code>assert n == 51</code> and the failure shows the values:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test">  ✗ tc: accepts the middle
      assert n == 51
             │ │
             │ false
             50
      cookbook-testing-unit.byn:17

◆ 4 passed · ✗ 1 failed · 0 pending · 0.47s
  rerun the failure: bayan test --only "accepts the middle"</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">def test_check_percent():
    with pytest.raises(RangeError):
        check_percent(101)</code></pre>
<p>Other test features in the spec (§20), such as <code>@pairwise</code>, <code>property</code>, <code>fuzz</code>, snapshots and doctests, are Planned. Doctests are marked "Not in v0.1". Check <code>bayan test --help</code> for what your build runs.</p>

<h2 id="benchmark">Benchmark a block of code</h2>
<p><strong>Planned</strong> for <code>bench</code> (§20, "Not in v0.1"). The spec form reports time per operation, allocations per operation and variance:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bench "parse ledger" { measure Ledger.read(sample) }</code></pre>
<p><strong>Runnable today:</strong> time a loop by hand with <code>Io.monotonicNanos()</code> and divide by the number of runs. One loop is a rough number, with no warm-up and no variance, so use it to compare orders of magnitude only.</p>
<example name="cookbook-testing-bench"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">func BenchmarkCollatz(b *testing.B) {
    for i := 0; i &lt; b.N; i++ { collatzSteps(27) }
}</code></pre>
